WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.761
The next command-line tool we are going to explore is netstat. Netstat displays network statistics, including open and listening ports and protocol statistics.

00:10.761 --> 00:22.080
I'm first going to run netstat /? and press Enter to see the different options for netstat.

00:22.080 --> 00:36.668
The three most common ones used are the -a, to display all connections and listening ports, -p, to specify connections by protocol, and -s, to display per-protocol statistics.

00:36.668 --> 00:47.291
Let's run through each one of these. Netstat -a displays open and listening ports.

00:47.291 --> 00:56.934
As I run this, notice that many of these port numbers that are returned are mostly dynamic ports, meaning port numbers of 49152 and up.

00:56.934 --> 01:01.031
These ports open when needed as connections are established.

01:01.031 --> 01:10.152
Now instead of waiting for this to finish, I'm going to press Ctrl+C to stop this, and then let's say I just want to see statistics for a single protocol.

01:10.152 --> 01:24.209
I'll type netstat -p and then tcp for the TCP protocol, I'll press Enter, and only the TCP information is going to display.

01:24.209 --> 01:46.754
And again, instead of waiting I'll stop the run here. Finally, to see per-protocol statistics, I'll type netstat -s, and this is exactly what we see: per-protocol statistics.

